WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.452
Data can look correct on the screen and still be stored incorrectly.

00:00:04.452 --> 00:00:09.802
In this example, order date looks like a date, and amount looks like a number.

00:00:09.802 --> 00:00:14.287
But both columns are initially stored as text.

00:00:14.287 --> 00:00:16.387
Click Run and look at the dtypes output.

00:00:16.387 --> 00:00:23.587
You will see that Pandas does not automatically know that these strings should be interpreted as dates or

00:00:23.587 --> 00:00:25.552
numeric values. Then look at info.

00:00:25.552 --> 00:00:32.577
The info method gives us a compact summary of the DataFrame, including column names, non-null counts,

00:00:32.577 --> 00:00:35.282
data types, and memory information.

00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:37.087
Why does this matter?

00:00:37.087 --> 00:00:44.182
If amount is stored as text, numerical operations may fail or produce unexpected behavior.

00:00:44.182 --> 00:00:51.232
If order date is stored as text, we cannot easily extract the month, weekday, or time difference.

00:00:51.232 --> 00:00:55.147
This is why checking data types is part of data quality work.

00:00:55.147 --> 00:00:59.622
Do not decide what a column is based only on how it looks.

00:00:59.622 --> 00:01:02.767
Ask Pandas what type it is actually using.

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:06.664
Later in the module, we will convert these values safely.
